This repository uses modular Cursor rules with scoped `.mdc` files (YAML frontmatter for name/priority/scope). See `.cursor/`:

1. 01-expertise-and-principles.mdc
2. 02-project-overview.mdc
3. 03-system-components-and-requirements.mdc
4. 04-project-structure.mdc
5. 05-code-style-and-structure.mdc
6. 06-performance-optimization.mdc
7. 07-project-conventions.mdc
8. 08-testing-and-deployment.mdc
9. 09-package-management.mdc
10. 10-entity-panel-matrix.mdc
11. 11-frontend-design.mdc
12. 12-ai-feature-lifecycle.mdc

After ANY code change (feature, fix, refactoring, review, etc.):
* Update the changelog in /changelogs/:
    - Use the version (vX.X.X) as the first level for storing the logs. 
        - Get and Use the version specified in the pyproject.toml file for each entry
        - Each application update must be affected to the right version/folder.
    - For the filename:
        - Use the user (github) as prefix: [user]
        - Then Use today's date as filename: YYYY-MM-DD.log 
        For example if use benoitcayladbx make an update on the version 0.2.1 the 2026 May 20th i should have the file /changelogs/v0.2.1/benoitcayladbx_2026-05-20.log)
    - If the file already exists, append a new section (one file per day).
    - If the file does not exist, create it.
    - Each section must include: a title, context, numbered list of changes
      with file paths and a short description, the list of modified files,
      and the test result.
* Run the tests and report results — use `uv run --frozen pytest -q -m "not scenario"` (the `--frozen` flag is mandatory: a bare `uv run` poisons `uv.lock` and breaks the next deploy). The `tests/e2e/scenarios/` suites are opt-in; run them only when explicitly asked (see `.cursor/08-testing-and-deployment`).

When asking for a code review, do all these tasks (in this order):
* Review all application code, and check that the rules defined in src/.coding_rules.md are respected
* Try to detect duplication code, and optimize if necessary
* Try to find not-used code, and remove the unnecessary code when possible, propose a plan instead
* Check all the session_data items to see if they are used and/or if they could be calculated as well
    - If they are useless, remove them.
    - If they can be calculated, optimize the code or propose a plan.
* Run the tests (after all changes made) — `uv run --frozen pytest -q -m "not scenario"` (scenarios are opt-in; see `.cursor/08-testing-and-deployment`)
* Update the documentation (in /docs, the sphinx documentation and the README.md)

When working on an AI / LLM agent feature (any change under `src/agents/**`,
`src/back/core/agents/**`, `src/mcp-server/**`, a new MLflow-traced LLM call, or
an MCP tool wrapping an agent):
* Follow the gate in `.cursor/12-ai-feature-lifecycle.mdc` (CNS §3.5) — the
  companion path of least resistance is `.claude/skills/ai-feature/SKILL.md`.
* No new agent ships without a `.planning/<slug>/SPEC.md`; no prompt/tool change
  ships without an eval delta + linked MLflow eval run. The SPEC.md + dataset is
  the entry ticket, and MLflow tracing must be present on every Foundation Model
  API code path.

When asking to deploy:
* **There is no in-repo deploy automation.** The Databricks Apps path, the asset
  bundle (`databricks.yml`, `app.yaml.template`) and `scripts/deploy*` were all
  removed in v0.7.1. `make deploy` does not exist — do not suggest it.
* OntoBricks is a container-ready ASGI process. `make prod` runs it the way a
  container should: `ONTOBRICKS_CONTAINERIZED=true python run.py`, which binds
  `0.0.0.0` on `$PORT`. `make run` / `make dev` bind loopback for local work.
* Deploying therefore means building an image around that entrypoint and running
  it on the target platform (Azure Container Apps, ECS, Kubernetes, …) with the
  environment documented in `.env.example`. See `README.md` §"Running in a
  container" for the required variables, the `documentation/` bundling
  requirement, and the **single-replica** constraint (APScheduler runs
  in-process; more than one replica duplicates every scheduled build).
* Before handing a build off: `uv run --frozen pytest -q -m "not scenario"` green
  and `uv.lock` free of proxy URLs — `.claude/skills/deploy/SKILL.md` has the
  lockfile checks, which still matter because the container installs from it.

When asking to create a Release Notes:
* Looks at the folder ./changelogs, and use the current version (vx.x.x)
* Build a quick summary about all the files (covering the version x.x.x) and use the file ./releases/ReleaseNotes_V0.2.0.md as template and build the current Release Notes by using the same structure.
* Creates the new Release file : releases/ReleaseNotes_Vx.x.x.md (by using the relevant version) 